    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d506">

                <pb facs="0925" n="821" xml:id="pg821"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">J. C. S. Files</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the United States Chiefs of
                    Staff</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/> C. C. S. 411/5</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cairo</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-12-07">7 December 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Operations in the Southeast Asia
                        Command</hi></p>
                <p>1. In order to settle the question of tonnage lift to China versus availability
                    of aircraft from A. T. C. India–China Wing, it is recommended that the Combined
                    Chiefs of Staff accept the following in lieu of the solution recommended in C.
                    C. S. 411/4, <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sextant</hi>, 5 December 1943:<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d506-f1"/></p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>. The Combined Chiefs of Staff recognize the
                    principle that the Supreme Allied Commander, Southeast Asia Command<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d506-f2"/> must have control over resources allocated to him for the
                    accomplishment of the assigned objectives.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>. The Combined Chiefs of Staff also recognize the
                    necessity of firm commitments of tonnage over the “hump” into China during the
                    next six months. The Combined Chiefs of Staff direct:</p>
                <p>(1) That the tonnage over the “hump” be maintained on the following basis of
                    transport plane allotments: <table cols="4" rend="width: 70%"
                        rendition="#center" rows="7">
                        <row>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell role="num"><hi rendition="#italic">C–87’s</hi></cell>
                            <cell role="num"><hi rendition="#italic">C–46’s</hi></cell>
                            <cell role="num"><hi rendition="#italic">Tons Delivered</hi></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>December</cell>
                            <cell role="num">40</cell>
                            <cell role="num">97</cell>
                            <cell role="num">8,858</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>January</cell>
                            <cell role="num">40</cell>
                            <cell role="num">107</cell>
                            <cell role="num">9,535</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>February</cell>
                            <cell role="num">48</cell>
                            <cell role="num">120</cell>
                            <cell role="num">11,066</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>March</cell>
                            <cell role="num">55</cell>
                            <cell role="num">25</cell>
                            <cell role="num">5,614</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>April</cell>
                            <cell role="num">52</cell>
                            <cell role="num">46</cell>
                            <cell role="num">6,716</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>May</cell>
                            <cell role="num">50</cell>
                            <cell role="num">96</cell>
                            <cell role="num">9,686</cell>
                        </row>
                    </table></p>
                <p>(2) They further direct that transport plane allotments to the Supreme Allied
                    Commander, Southeast Asia Command, for <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tarzan</hi>,
                    be on the following basis: <list>
                        <item>15 Dec to 31 Jan—18 C–47’s or 12 C–46’s</item>
                        <item>1 Feb to 28 Feb—11 C–47’s or 8 C–46’s</item>
                        <item>1 Mar to 31 Mar—188 C–47’s or 126 C–46’s</item>
                        <item>1 Apr to 15 Apr—183 C–47’s or 122 C–46’s</item>
                        <item>16 Apr to 15 May—114 C–47’s or 76 C–46’s</item>
                        <item>16 May to 30 Jun—43 C–47’s or 29 C–46’s</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Note</hi>: The above subparagraph is based on the
                    assumption that 35 additional C–47’s will be available in the theater by 1
                    February. (Now allotted to the 10th Air Force)</p>
                <pb facs="0926" n="822" xml:id="pg822"/>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>. The Combined Chiefs of Staff direct that any
                    shortages in delivery of transport aircraft into the theater on present
                    allotment bases be prorated in proportion to the allotments outlined in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi> (1) and (2) above. Excess in numbers of A. T. C.
                    aircraft over the expectations outlined in (1) and (2) above will be allocated
                    by direction of the Commanding General, U. S. A. A. F., India,<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d506-f3"/> during the above period.</p>
            </div>
